# 3. CURRENT BUSINESS WORKFLOW

## 3.1 Existing manual process

The existing carwash process works approximately as follows:

1. A vehicle arrives at the carwash.
2. A supervisor conducts a walk-through or inspection.
3. Details are recorded on a paper sheet.
4. The supervisor records the vehicle registration number.
5. The supervisor records the services or vehicle areas to be cleaned.
6. The supervisor records the amount charged.
7. The vehicle type is recorded.
8. Cleaners assigned to the vehicle are recorded.
9. Cleaner commission is calculated.
10. Payment method is recorded.
11. Paper sheets are transferred to accounts after approximately one to two days.
12. Accounts personnel manually enter the information into Excel.

The system should digitize this workflow and make information available in real time.

## 3.2 Operational volumes

Use these figures when designing performance and usability:

- More than 100 vehicles on a normal weekday.
- Approximately 200 vehicles on Saturdays.
- Lower volumes on Sundays, Mondays and Tuesdays.
- Maximum approximately two supervisors per shift.
- At least eight cleaners per shift.
- Daytime staffing can reach approximately fifteen cleaners.
- Staff members have smartphones.
- The business also has desktop computers.

The system must be efficient on mobile devices, especially for supervisors recording vehicle services and assigning cleaners.

